## 1. Design Summary

Problem: GateGuard's per-file first-touch gate requires a submit-twice loop
for every new file path. For lore-dump sessions (20+ files, same risk tier,
same directory), this is excessive friction with no additional security value.

Solution: A user-issued batch authorization directive in the chat transcript.
Because Claude writes as `role: "assistant"` in the JSONL and the hook only
reads `role: "user"` entries, Claude cannot self-authorize. Only text
physically typed by the user (role: "user") is honored.

Directive syntax (in user chat message):
  GATEGUARD-BATCH-AUTH: glob=docs/minecraft-lore/biomes/* ops=Write,Edit ext=.md max=20 ttl=3600 risk=plain-markdown-lore-docs

Fields:
  glob=   Project-relative glob. No `..`, no shell metacharacters.
  ops=    Comma-separated: Write, Edit, Read (default: Write,Edit)
  ext=    Required extension filter (.md, .json, etc.) — must start with dot
  max=    Max file writes covered (integer, 1–200)
  ttl=    Seconds the auth is valid after session start (default: 3600)
  risk=   Free-text label the user attaches. Not validated; for audit trail.

All fields except `glob` are optional. Without ext=, any extension is covered
(not recommended — users should be explicit).

